  "claimBoundary": "Fourteen frozen decision-point cases, two DeepSeek families and two replicas. Historical oracle uses pre-existing HD2-N2 source-locked classification; Ordivon P2 uses later owner-native consumption. This establishes neither a universal retention policy nor that historical future success was foreseeable at t0. Invalid structured/provider outcomes are apparatus outcomes, not semantic misses. Ordinary archival is excluded from PRESERVE_OPTION.",
  "interpretation": {
    "genericOptionAwareRetentionPolicySupported": false,
    "historicalLatentOptionRealitySupported": true,
    "modelInteraction": "Flash is already very strong under GREEDY_CURRENT and OPTION_AWARE slightly reduces positive recall; Pro benefits directionally on backpropagation and one Engelbart replica, but still misses Richardson and P2. A retained option lens is therefore model/task conditional, consistent with H-PRIOR-BIAS.",
    "nextFalsifier": "Do not tune OPTION_AWARE v2 on these cases. If revisited, use a prospective capability portfolio where future workloads are hidden at t0 and then sampled from a precommitted distribution, allowing realized regret/carrying cost to be measured. For the current PAL third wave, move to complementarity/interactional credit because single-change marginal evaluation is still untested.",
    "reason": "Latent option value is independently recurrent in the pre-existing historical corpus and P2 supplies a real Ordivon later-consumption example, so zero-current-use is not equivalent to zero future value. But the preregistered OPTION_AWARE rule does not reliably identify that value at decision time: overall strict both-valid comparison is 4 wins / 3 losses / 35 ties (sign p=1.0), and development is exactly 2/2/16. Holdout valid accuracy is directionally higher (23/25 versus 20/25), driven mainly by Pro backpropagation decisions, but strict holdout pairs are only 2 wins / 1 loss / 19 ties. The policy also misses the most important owner-native P2 scoped-egress positive in every valid OPTION_AWARE run.",
    "specificityObservation": "Both treatments achieve 100% specificity on every valid negative control. The failure mode is not speculative over-retention; it is inability to identify which apparently dormant positive deserves carrying. This means the hard problem is option discovery/valuation under uncertainty, not merely adding a conservative option-value checklist.",
    "strongerCandidate": "Option value is a real outcome dimension, but decision-time option preservation likely requires explicit estimates of carrier cost, reacquisition cost, adjacency/branch probability and perhaps portfolio-level diversity rather than a binary generic rule. More importantly, these quantities may be fundamentally uncertain and should be represented as uncertainty/reopen triggers rather than forced retain/delete certainty."
  },
